Instead of starting with the most profitable job, this version fills the schedule from the last slot toward the first. For each slot, a max heap contains jobs that are allowed to finish in that slot or later. The most profitable available job is selected.

A job is added when its deadline reaches the current slot. Once selected, it is removed from the heap, so it cannot be scheduled twice.

```javascript
class Solution {
    JobScheduling(Jobs) {
        if (Jobs.length === 0) {
            return [0, 0];
        }

        const jobs = Jobs.slice().sort((a, b) => b[1] - a[1]);
        const maxDeadline = Math.max(...jobs.map(job => job[1]));
        const heap = [];
        let index = 0;
        let count = 0;
        let totalProfit = 0;

        const push = (job) => {
            heap.push(job);
            let child = heap.length - 1;

            while (child > 0) {
                const parent = Math.floor((child - 1) / 2);
                if (heap[parent][2] >= heap[child][2]) {
                    break;
                }
                [heap[parent], heap[child]] = [heap[child], heap[parent]];
                child = parent;
            }
        };

        const pop = () => {
            const top = heap[0];
            const last = heap.pop();

            if (heap.length > 0) {
                heap[0] = last;
                let parent = 0;

                while (true) {
                    let largest = parent;
                    const left = parent * 2 + 1;
                    const right = parent * 2 + 2;

                    if (left < heap.length && heap[left][2] > heap[largest][2]) {
                        largest = left;
                    }
                    if (right < heap.length && heap[right][2] > heap[largest][2]) {
                        largest = right;
                    }
                    if (largest === parent) {
                        break;
                    }

                    [heap[parent], heap[largest]] = [heap[largest], heap[parent]];
                    parent = largest;
                }
            }

            return top;
        };

        for (let slot = maxDeadline; slot >= 1; slot--) {
            while (index < jobs.length && jobs[index][1] >= slot) {
                push(jobs[index]);
                index++;
            }

            if (heap.length > 0) {
                const chosen = pop();
                count++;
                totalProfit += chosen[2];
            }
        }

        return [count, totalProfit];
    }
}
```

For a small dry run, at slot 3 all jobs with deadline at least 3 enter the heap. The largest profit is taken there. At slot 2, jobs with deadline 2 are added and another best job is selected. This leaves earlier slots for the remaining jobs.

If every deadline is zero or negative, no slot is processed and the result is `[0, 0]`. The original `Jobs` array is copied before sorting.

Time complexity: `O(n log n + D log n)`, where `D` is the largest deadline. Space complexity: `O(n)`.

Here the jobs are processed by increasing deadline. A min heap stores the profits of the jobs currently selected. If too many jobs fit before the current deadline, the smallest profit is removed. This keeps the largest possible profits while keeping the schedule valid.

The heap size is also the number of scheduled jobs. For instance, when the current deadline is 2, the heap can contain at most two jobs.

```java
import java.util.Arrays;
import java.util.PriorityQueue;

class Solution {
    public int[] JobScheduling(int[][] Jobs) {
        if (Jobs.length == 0) {
            return new int[] {0, 0};
        }

        Arrays.sort(Jobs, (a, b) -> Integer.compare(a[1], b[1]));
        PriorityQueue<Integer> selectedProfits = new PriorityQueue<>();

        for (int[] job : Jobs) {
            int deadline = Math.max(0, job[1]);
            if (deadline == 0) {
                continue;
            }

            selectedProfits.offer(job[2]);

            if (selectedProfits.size() > deadline) {
                selectedProfits.poll();
            }
        }

        int totalProfit = 0;
        for (int value : selectedProfits) {
            totalProfit += value;
        }

        return new int[] {selectedProfits.size(), totalProfit};
    }
}
```

An important edge case is a deadline of zero. Such a job has no available slot, so it is skipped. With jobs ending at deadlines 1, 2, and 2, the heap never keeps more than two jobs.

Sorting changes the order of the input rows, but the returned values are not affected.

Time complexity: `O(n log n)`. Space complexity: `O(n)` for the heap.

This version uses a small disjoint set structure to find the next free slot quickly. After using slot `x`, the parent of `x` points to the next slot that may still be free. That avoids scanning many occupied slots again.

Jobs are sorted by profit, so a more valuable job is always tried before a less valuable one.

```cpp
#include <algorithm>
#include <vector>
using namespace std;

class Solution{
    vector<int> parent;

    int findSlot(int slot) {
        if (parent[slot] == slot) {
            return slot;
        }
        return parent[slot] = findSlot(parent[slot]);
    }

  public:
    vector<int> JobScheduling(vector<vector<int>>& Jobs) {
        if (Jobs.empty()) {
            return {0, 0};
        }

        sort(Jobs.begin(), Jobs.end(), [](const vector<int>& a, const vector<int>& b) {
            return a[2] > b[2];
        });

        int largestDeadline = 0;
        for (const auto& job : Jobs) {
            largestDeadline = max(largestDeadline, job[1]);
        }

        parent.resize(largestDeadline + 1);
        for (int i = 0; i <= largestDeadline; ++i) {
            parent[i] = i;
        }

        int scheduled = 0;
        int profit = 0;

        for (const auto& job : Jobs) {
            int deadline = min(job[1], largestDeadline);
            int slot = findSlot(deadline);

            if (slot > 0) {
                ++scheduled;
                profit += job[2];
                parent[slot] = findSlot(slot - 1);
            }
        }

        return {scheduled, profit};
    }
};
```

The parent array has a special slot 0, which means that no usable slot is left. Deadlines larger than the number of jobs are harmless because only the needed range is created. The job list is sorted in place.

Time complexity: `O(n log n + n alpha(D))`, usually written as `O(n log n)`. Space complexity: `O(D)`, where `D` is the largest deadline.

This one treats a schedule as a choice of jobs for time slots. After sorting by deadline, `best[t]` stores the best pair for scheduling exactly up to `t` jobs. The pair compares the number of jobs first, then the profit, so the result keeps the largest possible schedule and the best profit for that size.

The loop goes backward over `t`. That prevents the current job from being used more than once.

```csharp
using System;
using System.Linq;

class Solution
{
    public int[] JobScheduling(int[][] Jobs)
    {
        if (Jobs.Length == 0)
        {
            return new int[] { 0, 0 };
        }

        int[][] jobs = Jobs
            .OrderBy(job => job[1])
            .ToArray();

        int n = jobs.Length;
        int[] bestCount = new int[n + 1];
        int[] bestProfit = new int[n + 1];

        foreach (int[] job in jobs)
        {
            int limit = Math.Min(Math.Max(job[1], 0), n);

            for (int time = limit; time >= 1; time--)
            {
                int candidateCount = bestCount[time - 1] + 1;
                int candidateProfit = bestProfit[time - 1] + job[2];

                if (candidateCount > bestCount[time] ||
                    (candidateCount == bestCount[time] && candidateProfit > bestProfit[time]))
                {
                    bestCount[time] = candidateCount;
                    bestProfit[time] = candidateProfit;
                }
            }
        }

        int answerCount = 0;
        int answerProfit = 0;

        for (int time = 0; time <= n; time++)
        {
            if (bestCount[time] > answerCount ||
                (bestCount[time] == answerCount && bestProfit[time] > answerProfit))
            {
                answerCount = bestCount[time];
                answerProfit = bestProfit[time];
            }
        }

        return new int[] { answerCount, answerProfit };
    }
}
```

A dry run with deadlines 1, 2, and 2 fills at most one job in the first slot and two jobs by the second slot. A job whose deadline is larger than `n` is limited to `n`, since there cannot be more than `n` scheduled jobs.

This is easier to follow than a slot-linking structure, but it uses more time for large input sizes. The input array is not changed.

Time complexity: `O(n log n + n^2)`. Space complexity: `O(n)`.

