## Intuition
The core objective is to maximize total profit within restricted time constraints, which naturally lends itself to a greedy approach. By sorting the jobs based on highest profit first, we prioritize the most lucrative opportunities. To ensure that these high-paying jobs do not block tighter deadlines, the ideal strategy is to schedule each selected job at its absolute latest possible time slot (its deadline).

## Approach
The algorithm copies and sorts the jobs in descending order of profit, breaking ties by choosing earlier deadlines first. It determines the total timeline size based on the maximum deadline and initializes a Disjoint Set Union (DSU) path-tracking array with a size of max_deadline + 1. This extra slot accommodates 1-indexed deadlines directly within JavaScript's 0-indexed arrays while leaving slot 0 to act as a natural dead-end marker. As the code iterates through the sorted jobs, a recursive DSU function instantly jumps to the next available free slot at or before the job's deadline in near O(1) time. If a valid slot greater than 0 is found, the job is scheduled, the counters are updated, and that slot is merged with the preceding one (slot - 1) to dynamically route subsequent lookups.

## Solution
```javascript[]
class Solution {
    JobScheduling(Jobs) {
        //your code goes here
        var jobs = [...Jobs];
        jobs.sort((a, b) => {
            if (a[2] === b[2]) {
                return a[1] - b[1];
            }
            return b[2] - a[2];
        });

        var len = jobs.length;
        var max_deadline = Math.max(...jobs.map(j => j[1]));

        var available_slot = Array.from({ length: max_deadline + 1}, (_, i) => i);
        var DSU = (i) => {
            if (available_slot[i] === i) {
                return i;
            }
            return available_slot[i] = DSU(available_slot[i]);
        };

        var job_count = 0;
        var total_profit = 0;
        var slot = 0;
        for (var i = 0; i < len; i++) {
            slot = DSU(jobs[i][1])
            if (slot > 0) {
                job_count++;
                total_profit += jobs[i][2];
                available_slot[slot] = slot - 1;
            }
        }

        return [job_count, total_profit];
    }
}
```

## Complexity
**Time Complexity:** 
O(n log n + n * α(m)) where n is the number of jobs and m is the maximum deadline. The sorting step takes O(n log n). The DSU init loop takes O(m), the mapping takes O(n), and the main loop runs n times with nearly constant time amortized DSU operations (inverse Ackermann function α). Overall complexity is dominated by sorting: O(n log n + m).

**Space Complexity:**
O(n + m) where n is the space for the sorted copy of jobs and m is the space for the available_slot array of size max_deadline.53
